{"kind":"assert","text":"work-loop.md is repaired and the scope question is still open. Progress at cf479712 on request/docset-citation-integrity.\n\nThe one repair your conditions name is done. work-loop.md rested on a comma-joined pseudo-path, a branch name and the whole-repository path — three anchors no merge can supersede, so the page could never flare however far the loop moved under it. It now rests on what the page itself says defines it: internal/workroom for kinds.go's lifecycle edges and satisfiers, internal/workroom/fold.go for the eight statuses and the promisor rule, and cmd/gs/main.go for the guarded commands. I did not choose those by subject-matter guess; the page has a section called Where this is defined and it names them. The gate now reports work-loop.md as stale and no longer fails it, which is the correct outcome and the distinction working.\n\nThe gate itself is finished. ClassifyCitation takes primitives rather than workroom types so every branch is exercisable without a workroom — the retired and unresolvable cases are otherwise tested only by whatever the live log happens to hold, and those are the ones that matter most. The gate calls the same function, so there is one set of rules and not a copy in the test. Verified by mutation: making staleness fatal, accepting branch-name paths, and dropping the retired check each fail the focused tests.\n\nThe scope question is unchanged and still yours. 32 fatal citations across 40 pages, down one from 41 because work-loop.md is fixed. I am not going to repair the other forty by inference, and I am not going to soften the gate to make them pass. The three options stand: a recorded baseline that must only shrink, repairing all forty here, or landing the new classes report-only — which I argued against, because a check that reports and never fails is the state that made green meaningless in the first place.\n\nOne thing I would add if you want it, arising from the abbreviated-commit incident rather than from this assignment: a citation whose artifact carries a commit that is not a full canonical object id should also fail. Such an artifact cannot participate in merge or review at all, since validateCheckout refuses short ids and resolveReviewed matches the commit as an exact string. It is a fifth classification, not a change to the four, and I have not added it because you did not ask for it."}